Best & Recommended Approach (2025–2026) → Use python-oracledb (native driver)

python-oracledb is the official successor to cx_Oracle and is strongly recommended for all new projects.
Option 1 – Easiest & Most Recommended (Thin mode – no Oracle client needed)
Python

# pip install oracledb

import oracledb

try:
    # Format:  user / password @ host : port / service_name
    connection = oracledb.connect(
        user="reports",
        password="2322",
        dsn="172.20.20.24:1213/misdb"
    )

    print("Successfully connected to Oracle!")
    print("Database version:", connection.version)

    # Example query
    with connection.cursor() as cursor:
        cursor.execute("SELECT * FROM dual")
        result = cursor.fetchone()
        print("Result from DUAL:", result)

except oracledb.Error as error:
    print("Error connecting to Oracle:", error)
finally:
    if 'connection' in locals():
        connection.close()

Advantages:

    No Oracle Instant Client installation needed
    Works very well in Docker, cloud functions, etc.
    Fast & lightweight

Option 2 – If you already have / want to use Oracle Client (Thick mode)
Python

import oracledb

oracledb.init_oracle_client()  # ← only needed if libraries are not in PATH

connection = oracledb.connect(
    user="reports",
    password="2322",
    dsn="172.20.20.24:1213/misdb"
)

If you really want to use the exact JDBC URL style

python-oracledb does not accept JDBC URLs directly.

You have two realistic options:
A. Parse the JDBC URL yourself (clean & simple)
Python

import oracledb
import re

jdbc_url = "jdbc:oracle:thin:@172.20.20.24:1213/misdb"

# Very simple parser for your format
match = re.match(r"jdbc:oracle:thin:@([^:]+):(\d+)/(.+)", jdbc_url)
if not match:
    raise ValueError("Unsupported JDBC URL format")

host, port, service_name = match.groups()

connection = oracledb.connect(
    user="reports",
    password="2322",
    dsn=f"{host}:{port}/{service_name}"
)

B. Use Java bridge → jaydebeapi + JDBC driver (much heavier)

Only do this if you must use JDBC URL / same driver as Java application.
Bash

pip install JayDeBeApi JPype1

Python

import jaydebeapi

jdbc_url = "jdbc:oracle:thin:@172.20.20.24:1213/misdb"
driver_class = "oracle.jdbc.OracleDriver"
jar_path = "/path/to/ojdbc11.jar"   # Download from Oracle (ojdbc11.jar or ojdbc8.jar)

conn = jaydebeapi.connect(
    driver_class,
    jdbc_url,
    ["reports", "2322"],
    jar_path
)

curs = conn.cursor()
curs.execute("SELECT * FROM dual")
print(curs.fetchall())

conn.close()

Downsides:

    Needs Java + JDBC driver jar
    Much slower
    Harder to deploy
    Not recommended unless you have very special requirements

