with select and insert query on table

Here are several practical examples of SELECT + INSERT patterns commonly used when working with Oracle databases (using Python + oracledb):
1. Most Common Pattern: Copy/Transform rows from one table to another
Python

import oracledb

try:
    connection = oracledb.connect(
        user="reports",
        password="2322",
        dsn="172.20.20.24:1213/misdb"
    )
    
    cursor = connection.cursor()

    # Pattern 1 - Simple copy with some transformation
    cursor.execute("""
        INSERT INTO target_table (
            id, 
            customer_name, 
            amount, 
            record_date, 
            status_flag
        )
        SELECT 
            id,
            UPPER(customer_name) as customer_name,
            amount * 1.18 as amount_with_tax,
            record_date,
            'PROCESSED' as status_flag
        FROM source_table
        WHERE record_date >= TRUNC(SYSDATE) - 30
          AND status = 'PENDING'
    """)

    connection.commit()
    print(f"Rows inserted: {cursor.rowcount}")

except oracledb.Error as error:
    print("Error:", error)
    connection.rollback()
finally:
    cursor.close()
    connection.close()

2. Insert from SELECT + some calculation/sequence
SQL

INSERT INTO daily_summary (
    summary_date,
    branch_id,
    total_sales,
    total_transactions,
    avg_transaction_value,
    created_at
)
SELECT 
    TRUNC(transaction_date) as summary_date,
    branch_id,
    SUM(sale_amount) as total_sales,
    COUNT(*) as total_transactions,
    ROUND(AVG(sale_amount), 2) as avg_transaction_value,
    SYSDATE as created_at
FROM transactions
WHERE transaction_date >= TRUNC(SYSDATE) - 7
GROUP BY TRUNC(transaction_date), branch_id

3. Insert only new/changed records (using MERGE or NOT EXISTS)
Python

# Using NOT EXISTS (simple & readable)
cursor.execute("""
    INSERT INTO target_history (id, name, amount, change_date)
    SELECT 
        s.id,
        s.name,
        s.amount,
        SYSDATE
    FROM source_table s
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1 
        FROM target_history t 
        WHERE t.id = s.id 
          AND t.change_date = TRUNC(SYSDATE)
    )
""")

Alternative using MERGE (very powerful & usually fastest):
SQL

MERGE INTO target_table t
USING (
    SELECT id, name, status, amount, last_modified
    FROM source_table
    WHERE last_modified > :last_run_time
) s
ON (t.id = s.id)
WHEN MATCHED THEN
    UPDATE SET
        t.status = s.status,
        t.amount = s.amount,
        t.last_modified = s.last_modified
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
    INSERT (id, name, status, amount, last_modified)
    VALUES (s.id, s.name, s.status, s.amount, s.last_modified)

Python version with bind variable:
Python

from datetime import datetime, timedelta

last_run = datetime.now() - timedelta(days=1)

cursor.execute("""
    MERGE INTO target_table t
    USING (
        SELECT id, name, status, amount, last_modified
        FROM source_table
        WHERE last_modified > :last_run
    ) s
    ON (t.id = s.id)
    WHEN MATCHED THEN
        UPDATE SET
            t.status = s.status,
            t.amount = s.amount,
            t.last_modified = s.last_modified
    WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
        INSERT (id, name, status, amount, last_modified)
        VALUES (s.id, s.name, s.status, s.amount, s.last_modified)
""", last_run=last_run)

Quick Reference Table - Popular Patterns
Goal	Recommended Approach	Performance	Complexity
Simple copy all rows	INSERT INTO ... SELECT *	★★★★★	★
Copy with filter + transformation	INSERT ... SELECT ... WHERE	★★★★	★★
Daily summary / aggregation	INSERT ... SELECT ... GROUP BY	★★★★	★★★
Upsert (update or insert)	MERGE statement	★★★★★	★★★★
Insert only new records	INSERT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS	★★★★	★★
Insert only changed records	MERGE or INSERT + NOT EXISTS	★★★★	★★★
Copy between schemas	INSERT INTO schema2.t SELECT * FROM schema1.t	★★★★	★

Which of these patterns is closest to what you want to achieve?

